---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import CopyBlock from '../components/CopyBlock.astro';
import BeginnerNote from '../components/BeginnerNote.astro';
---

<MainLayout title="Security & Privacy for Business" subtitle="Keeping your data safe when using AI tools" module={9}>

  <!-- 1. Intro -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">This module is not optional</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      AI tools are powerful, but with power comes responsibility. This module covers what you need to know to use Claude Code safely in a business context. This isn't optional — this is essential.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Whether you're working with customer data, internal tools, or client projects, the habits you build here will protect you, your company, and the people whose data you handle.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. What goes where: Local vs API -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">What goes where: Local vs API</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Understanding what stays on your machine and what leaves it is the foundation of using Claude Code safely.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-5 space-y-4">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-2 w-2 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>When you type to Claude Code,</strong> your messages go to Anthropic's API servers for processing. This is how Claude "thinks" — it happens in the cloud, not on your machine.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-2 w-2 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>Your files are read locally</strong> — Claude Code reads them on your machine and sends relevant content as context with your messages.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-2 w-2 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>Generated code appears locally</strong> on your machine. Claude writes files directly to your project folder.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-2 w-2 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>Anthropic's data retention policy:</strong> on the paid API, your messages are not used for training. They are processed and discarded, not stored for model improvement.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <BeginnerNote>
      Think of it like a phone call with an expert. You tell them about your project, they give advice, but they don't keep a recording of the call.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 2.5. Permission Model Deep Dive -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">Permission Model Deep Dive</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Claude Code's permission system uses a three-tier model: <strong>Deny → Ask → Allow</strong>. The first match wins. This gives you precise control over what Claude can and can't do.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-5">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text mb-3">How it works:</p>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-danger"></span>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text">Deny (block dangerous actions)</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-1">Certain operations are blocked entirely — force push to main branch, rm -rf, editing sensitive auth files.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-orange"></span>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text">Ask (check with you)</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-1">Most actions default to asking. Claude shows you what it wants to do and waits for your approval.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
          <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-success"></span>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text">Allow (trust automatically)</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-1">Safe actions like reading files or creating test files can be whitelisted to skip approval.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-5 text-sm font-semibold text-text">Configure this in <code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-purple">.claude/settings.json</code>:</p>

    <CopyBlock
      command={`{\n  "permissions": {\n    "deny": [\n      "git push --force",\n      "rm -rf",\n      "edit .env*",\n      "edit **/*auth*.ts"\n    ],\n    "ask": [\n      "npm install",\n      "git commit",\n      "run test"\n    ],\n    "allow": [\n      "read **/*.ts",\n      "grep",\n      "list files"\n    ]\n  }\n}`}
      breakdown={[
        { segment: '"deny"', explanation: 'Operations that are completely blocked' },
        { segment: '"ask"', explanation: 'Default behavior — Claude asks before proceeding' },
        { segment: '"allow"', explanation: 'Safe operations that auto-approve' },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="mt-5 rounded-lg border border-purple-200 bg-purple-50 p-4">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-purple">Enterprise Controls (2026)</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-text-secondary">
        Large organizations can configure <strong>managed-settings.json</strong> for team-wide policies: zero data retention, SSO integration, audit logging, and pre-approved action whitelists.
      </p>
    </div>

    <BeginnerNote>
      Permissions are your safety net. Deny blocks the truly dangerous stuff, Ask checks with you on important operations, and Allow speeds things up for routine tasks. This layered approach keeps you safe without slowing you down.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 2.6. Sandboxing -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">Sandboxing</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Claude Code can run in <strong>sandboxed mode</strong> — a restricted environment where Claude has limited filesystem and network access. This is OS-level protection on top of permissions.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-5 space-y-3">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>Restricted filesystem:</strong> Claude can only access files inside your project folder. It can't read your Documents, browse your Downloads, or access system files.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>No network access:</strong> Claude can't reach external services, APIs, or the internet — only tools your project provides.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>Complements permissions:</strong> Sandboxing is OS-level protection. Permissions are tool-level control. Together they provide belt-and-suspenders safety.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-5 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      Best for: CI/CD pipelines, automated workflows, untrusted environments, or when you want Claude to work on sensitive projects without any risk of it escaping the project folder.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-5 rounded-lg border border-success/30 bg-success-light p-4">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-success">When to enable sandboxing</p>
      <ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-sm text-text-secondary">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
          <span class="text-success font-bold text-xs mt-0.5">●</span>
          <span>Running Claude on CI/CD servers with access to production credentials</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
          <span class="text-success font-bold text-xs mt-0.5">●</span>
          <span>Working on client projects where isolation is required</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
          <span class="text-success font-bold text-xs mt-0.5">●</span>
          <span>Testing untrusted code or scripts</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <BeginnerNote>
      Sandboxing means Claude can only touch files in your project folder and can't access the internet. It's belt and suspenders — permissions say what Claude can do, sandboxing says where it can do it.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. What NOT to paste into Claude -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-danger/30 bg-danger-light p-6 md:p-8">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
      <span class="text-lg">&#9888;</span>
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-danger">What NOT to paste into Claude</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Even though Anthropic doesn't use your data for training, you should still treat any AI tool as an external service. Never send anything you wouldn't want in a data breach.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-5 space-y-3">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded-lg border border-danger/20 bg-surface p-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 text-danger font-bold text-sm shrink-0">&#10005;</span>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text">API keys and secrets</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-0.5">e.g., <code class="rounded bg-danger-light px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-danger">ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...</code></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded-lg border border-danger/20 bg-surface p-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 text-danger font-bold text-sm shrink-0">&#10005;</span>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text">Database passwords and connection strings</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-0.5">e.g., <code class="rounded bg-danger-light px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-danger">postgres://user:password@host:5432/db</code></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded-lg border border-danger/20 bg-surface p-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 text-danger font-bold text-sm shrink-0">&#10005;</span>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text">Customer personal information</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-0.5">Names, emails, phone numbers, addresses</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded-lg border border-danger/20 bg-surface p-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 text-danger font-bold text-sm shrink-0">&#10005;</span>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text">Internal business credentials</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-0.5">Pipedrive keys, Google API keys, Stripe secrets</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded-lg border border-danger/20 bg-surface p-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 text-danger font-bold text-sm shrink-0">&#10005;</span>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text">Financial data</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-0.5">Credit card numbers, bank details, account numbers</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded-lg border border-danger/20 bg-surface p-3">
        <span class="mt-0.5 text-danger font-bold text-sm shrink-0">&#10005;</span>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-text">Employee personal information</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-0.5">TFNs, payroll data, private HR records</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5 rounded-lg border border-danger/30 bg-surface p-4">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-danger">The golden rule:</p>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-text-secondary">
        If it would be bad in a data breach, don't paste it into any AI tool.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. .claudeignore — Your safety net -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text"><code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-2 py-0.5 font-mono text-purple">.claudeignore</code> — Your safety net</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      A <code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-purple">.claudeignore</code> file tells Claude Code to never read certain files or folders. Even if you accidentally ask Claude to look at a sensitive file, it will skip anything listed in this file.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-text-secondary">
      Create this file in the root of your project:
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command={`.env\n.env.*\n*.pem\n*.key\ncredentials/\nsecrets/\nnode_modules/`}
      breakdown={[
        { segment: ".env", explanation: "your main environment variables file (API keys, passwords)" },
        { segment: ".env.*", explanation: "any variant env files (.env.local, .env.production, etc.)" },
        { segment: "*.pem", explanation: "SSL/TLS certificate files" },
        { segment: "*.key", explanation: "private key files" },
        { segment: "credentials/", explanation: "any folder named credentials and everything inside it" },
        { segment: "secrets/", explanation: "any folder named secrets and everything inside it" },
        { segment: "node_modules/", explanation: "third-party packages — not sensitive, but massive and unnecessary" }
      ]}
    />

    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      Save this as <code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-purple">.claudeignore</code> in your project root (same level as <code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-purple">package.json</code>). Claude Code checks this file automatically before reading anything.
    </p>

    <BeginnerNote>
      This is like putting a "DO NOT OPEN" label on sensitive folders. Claude will skip them entirely — it won't even peek inside.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. Voice-to-Text: Local vs Cloud -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">Voice-to-Text: Local vs Cloud Transcription</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      If you use voice-to-text to talk to Claude Code, <strong>where your audio goes matters</strong>. Not all transcription tools are equal when it comes to privacy.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
      <!-- Local tools - success themed -->
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-success/30 bg-success-light p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-success font-bold">&#10003;</span>
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-success">Fully Local Apps (Recommended)</p>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-text-muted">e.g., GhostPepper, macOS Dictation, Whisper.cpp</p>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-2">
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">Runs transcription <strong>entirely on your machine</strong>.</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">Audio never leaves your device.</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">Your conversations stay completely private.</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">No third-party servers involved at any stage.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Cloud tools - danger themed -->
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-danger/30 bg-danger-light p-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-danger font-bold">&#10005;</span>
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-danger">Cloud-Based Tools (NOT Recommended)</p>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-text-muted">e.g., WhisperFlow, Otter.ai, cloud speech APIs</p>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-2">
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">Sends audio to <strong>external cloud services</strong> for processing.</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">Your voice data travels through third-party servers.</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">You lose control of that data once it leaves your machine.</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">Company discussions, client details, and internal plans could be exposed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-5 text-sm font-semibold text-text-secondary">
      For any business use, always choose tools that keep data local. If audio leaves your machine, it's a risk you don't need to take.
    </p>

    <BeginnerNote>
      <p>The key question to ask about any voice tool: <strong>"Does my audio stay on my computer, or does it get sent somewhere?"</strong> If it gets sent to a server — even for a split second — that's a cloud tool. For work conversations, always pick the local option.</p>
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Environment variables -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">Environment variables</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      Environment variables are secrets stored in a <code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-purple">.env</code> file that your code reads at runtime. They never get committed to git — they stay on your machine (or your server).
    </p>

    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      Here's what a typical <code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-purple">.env</code> file looks like:
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command={`SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co\nSUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...\nSTRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_...\nPIPEDRIVE_API_KEY=abc123...`}
      breakdown={[
        { segment: "SUPABASE_URL", explanation: "the address of your Supabase project" },
        { segment: "SUPABASE_ANON_KEY", explanation: "public-safe key for client-side Supabase access" },
        { segment: "STRIPE_SECRET_KEY", explanation: "Stripe payment processing key — server-only" },
        { segment: "PIPEDRIVE_API_KEY", explanation: "CRM integration key" }
      ]}
    />

    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      The critical step: make sure <code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-purple">.env</code> is in your <code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-purple">.gitignore</code> so it never gets pushed to GitHub:
    </p>

    <CopyBlock
      command={`echo ".env" >> .gitignore`}
      breakdown={[
        { segment: "echo", explanation: "print text" },
        { segment: '".env"', explanation: "the text to add — the filename to ignore" },
        { segment: ">>", explanation: "append to the end of a file (>> adds, > overwrites)" },
        { segment: ".gitignore", explanation: "the file that tells git what NOT to track" }
      ]}
    />

    <BeginnerNote>
      Environment variables are like writing a password on a sticky note under your keyboard — your app knows where to look, but the password is not written in the code itself. Anyone reading your code won't see the actual values.
    </BeginnerNote>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. Company policy recommendations -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-6 md:p-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text">Company policy recommendations</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-text-secondary">
      If you're using Claude Code in a team or business setting, here are the policies that should be non-negotiable:
    </p>

    <div class="mt-5 space-y-3">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>Always use <code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-purple">.claudeignore</code></strong> for sensitive directories — credentials, secrets, private keys.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>Never commit <code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-purple">.env</code> files</strong> — add them to <code class="rounded bg-purple-100 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs font-mono text-purple">.gitignore</code> on day one.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>Review all Claude-generated code</strong> before deploying to production. Claude is good, but it's not infallible.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>Keep customer data out</strong> of AI tool conversations. Use dummy data or anonymized samples when testing.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>Use local-only tools</strong> (GhostPepper, macOS Dictation, Whisper.cpp) over cloud-based alternatives (WhisperFlow, Otter.ai) for voice transcription.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
        <span class="mt-1 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-purple"></span>
        <p class="text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <strong>Maintain an audit trail:</strong> who used Claude, on what project, when. This is basic accountability.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-purple-200 bg-purple-50 p-5">
      <p class="text-lg font-bold text-purple">When in doubt, keep it out.</p>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-text-secondary">
        If you're not sure whether something is safe to share with an AI tool, the answer is don't. Use placeholder values, anonymized data, or describe the structure without including real values. It takes five extra seconds and eliminates the risk entirely.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Up next -->
  <section class="rounded-xl border border-purple/20 bg-purple-50 p-6 md:p-8">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-purple">Up next</p>
    <h2 class="mt-1 text-xl font-bold text-text">Module 10: Uses Beyond Code</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-text-secondary">
      Claude Code isn't just for developers. In the final module, we'll show you all the non-coding things it can do — from data wrangling to presentations to browser automation.
    </p>
    <a
      href="/10-beyond-code"
      class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm font-semibold text-purple hover:underline"
    >
      Continue to Module 10 &rarr;
    </a>
  </section>

</MainLayout>
